WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.270
Protocols, file formats, serialized
messages, databases,

2
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.205
and FFI often impose numeric
representations that differ from internal Rust types.

3
00:00:07.285 --> 00:00:10.761
A field may be fixed-width, signed,
unsigned, little-endian, big-endian,

4
00:00:10.841 --> 00:00:13.284
optional, versioned, or measured in a
specific unit.

5
00:00:13.364 --> 00:00:15.591
Those details are part of the security
contract.

6
00:00:15.671 --> 00:00:18.658
Binary formats and protocol fields should
be reviewed for width,

7
00:00:18.738 --> 00:00:20.964
signedness, endianness, unit, and valid
range.

8
00:00:21.044 --> 00:00:23.759
A length field in bytes is different from
a count of records.

9
00:00:23.839 --> 00:00:26.555
A version field is different from a
user-controlled limit.

10
00:00:26.635 --> 00:00:30.273
A signed external value may not safely map
to an unsigned internal value without

11
00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:30.950
validation.

12
00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.017
Serialization boundaries should validate
before internal use.

13
00:00:34.097 --> 00:00:37.736
Deserializing a number into a Rust type
does not automatically mean the value is

14
00:00:37.816 --> 00:00:38.956
valid for the operation.

15
00:00:39.036 --> 00:00:41.045
A database row, JSON field, binary
message,

16
00:00:41.125 --> 00:00:44.872
or configuration file can still carry a
value outside the domain's allowed range.

17
00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:48.265
Cross-language boundaries add another
layer because C, platform APIs,

18
00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:52.092
device SDKs, and native libraries may use
different integer widths or signedness

19
00:00:52.172 --> 00:00:52.824
assumptions.

20
00:00:52.904 --> 00:00:56.325
A type that looks like a length in one
language may not match the size or range

21
00:00:56.405 --> 00:00:57.219
expected by Rust.

22
00:00:57.299 --> 00:00:59.254
FFI wrappers should document the mapping.

23
00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:03.299
Unsafe and FFI wrappers should translate
external numbers into validated Rust domain

24
00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:04.628
types as early as possible.

25
00:01:04.708 --> 00:01:07.477
Document valid ranges, size relationships,
length fields,

26
00:01:07.557 --> 00:01:10.870
allocation limits, conversion behavior,
and ownership implications.

27
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.556
The more clearly the wrapper defines
numeric contracts,

28
00:01:13.636 --> 00:01:15.700
the less every caller has to rediscover
them.
